# JobU configuration

`jobud` works without an INI file, using its compiled defaults. The installed
example is guidance; it is **not loaded automatically**. Installation never
creates an active configuration.

This build uses these paths:

- Example: `@CMAKE_INSTALL_FULL_DATADIR@/jobu/jobud.ini.example`
- Active configuration: `@JB_JOBU_SYSCONFDIR@/jobud.ini`
- State directory: `@JB_JOBU_STATEDIR@`
- Runtime directory: `@JB_JOBU_RUNDIR@`

The Runtime component creates missing normal prefix directories: `etc` and
`var` with mode 0755, `var/lib` and `var/run` with mode 0700. Each operational
directory is created only when its configured path equals that normal default.
Existing directories retain their ownership, permissions and contents. Custom
paths require explicit provisioning. A root install does not choose a daemon
account; dedicated-account and Linux systemd deployments still need their
documented account/directory provisioning. macOS uses an ordinary user and
foreground operation.

To create an optional initial configuration, run the following command. It
refuses an existing destination, including a symlink, and creates the new file
with owner-only permissions. The restrictive umask applies only inside this
subshell. Provision the configuration directory first if it is external.

```sh
(
    example=@JOBU_README_EXAMPLE_SHELL@
    active=@JOBU_README_CONFIG_SHELL@
    if [ -e "$active" ] || [ -L "$active" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "Configuration already exists: $active" >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    umask 077
    set -C
    cat "$example" > "$active"
)
```

Edit the copy, then validate it with the installed daemon:

```sh
@JOBU_README_DAEMON_SHELL@ --check-config --config @JOBU_README_CONFIG_SHELL@
```

Explicit `--config` requires its file. Checking configuration does not create
runtime resources and does not prove that every later startup operation will
succeed. After validation, start normally at the configured prefix:

```sh
@JOBU_README_DAEMON_SHELL@
```

Reinstallation updates this guide and the example, but never opens, replaces or
changes the active INI, database/sidecars, endpoint locks or sockets. Review new
example settings manually; there is no automatic configuration merge.

`DESTDIR` stages installation only. `cmake --install --prefix` and relocating a
TGZ change installation destinations; they do not rewrite compiled defaults or
the paths above. No-argument startup applies at the configured prefix. At a
different location, explicitly configure the intended protected resources.

For a Linux Runtime TGZ upgrade, stop the daemon and verify the archive checksum
first. Use GNU tar's directory-preserving extraction policy:

```sh
tar --extract --gzip --file /path/to/runtime.tar.gz \
    --directory /path/to/existing-prefix --no-overwrite-dir
```

This preserves existing directory metadata, including an administrative 0750
runtime directory. Ordinary tar extraction can reapply archived directory modes;
arbitrary extraction commands do not carry this preservation guarantee. Archives
contain the example/guide and empty private layout, never an active INI or live
database. Installation/extraction does not activate services or start a daemon.
